⸻

1) Overview — full architecture (concrete)

Screenshot (H × W × 3)
  ↓ (patchify)
ViT‑G/14 (frozen or finetuned)  →  patch embeddings  (N_patches × D_v)
  ↓
Q‑Former (learnable query tokens) — cross‑attends to ViT outputs
  ↓
Linear projection (Q‑former_dim → LLM_embed_dim)
  ↓
Prefix embeddings (K query embeddings) fed into LLM decoder as image prefix
  ↓
LLM (causal / encoder‑decoder) → autoregressive generation of JSON/XML tokens

Components I recommend for maximum accuracy (no size constraint):
	•	Vision encoder: laion/CLIP-ViT-g-14 (ViT‑G/14 weights).  ￼ ￼
	•	Bridge / projection: Q‑Former (32–64 learnable queries, 12 Transformer layers, hidden dim ≈ 1408 or 2048) — same concept as BLIP‑2.  ￼ ￼
	•	Projector: single Linear layer mapping Q‑Former output dimension → LLM embed dim (e.g. 4096 for T5‑XXL or 5120+ for Llama2 large variants).
	•	LLM / decoder: T5‑XXL (encoder‑decoder) or a causal LLM like Llama‑2‑13B / 70B (if you prefer causal modeling). For best structure handling, T5‑XXL (encoder‑decoder) or Llama2‑70B give top performance (choose based on available infra).
	•	Training adapters: use LoRA on LLM layers + train Q‑Former (recommended) so you only train a small fraction of params.  ￼ ￼

⸻

2) What each component does (practical specifics)
	1.	ViT‑G/14 (vision encoder)
	•	Input: resized screenshot (recommend 1024×1024 or 1344×1344 for fine UI detail).
	•	Output: N × D_v patch embeddings (e.g., if 14×14 patches at 1024 → ~ (1024/14)² ≈ 538 patches). ViT‑G is wide/deep so it captures tiny UI details. Use pretrained OpenCLIP ViT‑G weights on HF.  ￼ ￼
	2.	Q‑Former (learnable queries)
	•	Learnable K query vectors (K = 32 or 64).
	•	Each Q‑former block: self‑attention over queries + cross‑attention to ViT patch embeddings + feedforward. Use ~12 blocks (can be 6–12). Implementation reference: LAVIS / BLIP‑2 Q‑former code.  ￼
	•	Output: K × D_q representations (semantic image summary tokens).
	3.	Linear projection (the actual projection layer)
	•	Single Linear(D_q → D_lm) that maps each of the K query outputs into the LLM embedding dimension. This is the projection you asked about — it turns Q‑former outputs into prefix embeddings understandable by the LLM.
	•	If LLM uses positional embeddings for inputs, add positional encodings suited to prefix length K (or feed with inputs_embeds carefully — see HF notes about inputs_embeds behaviour).  ￼
	4.	LLM / Text Decoder
	•	Feed the projected K vectors as prefix embeddings to the LLM, then continue token generation conditioned on that prefix.
	•	If using encoder‑decoder (T5): you can either (A) treat projected queries as additional encoder tokens (preferred — add them to encoder input), or (B) use them as prefix to decoder (less standard for T5). Many BLIP‑2/T5 variants prepend them to LLM context. LAVIS’ BLIP2‑T5 integration is a direct reference.  ￼
	5.	LoRA / Adapters
	•	Do not fine‑tune entire LLM. Attach LoRA adapters to attention & MLP projections (e.g. q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, fc1) and train LoRA + Q‑former. This gives excellent accuracy vs compute tradeoff.
	6.	Output
	•	LLM generates tokenized JSON (or XML). Use constrained decoding / schema-checker postprocess to guarantee valid JSON/XML.

⸻

3) Exact recommended dimensions & hyper‑choices (concrete)
	•	Image size: 1344×1344 (letterbox preserving aspect ratio) — keeps small UI text legible.
	•	ViT‑G patch size: 14 (fixed). Use HF ViT‑G weights.  ￼
	•	Q‑Former: K = 32 (start) or 64 (better), layers = 12, heads = 16, hidden_dim D_q = 1408 (match ViT hidden size or 1024–2048).  ￼
	•	Projector: Linear(D_q, D_lm) (e.g., Linear(1408, 4096) for T5‑XXL)
	•	LLM: T5‑XXL (11B) or Llama‑2‑13B/70B. If you want best accuracy and can host 70B, pick Llama‑2‑70B + LoRA (or full fine‑tune).
	•	LoRA ranks: r = 64 for large LLMs (adjust down if memory constrained).
	•	Batch & LR: LR Q‑former + LoRA 1e‑4 – 2e‑4, AdamW, weight decay 0.01, warmup 2–5% steps. Use mixed precision (fp16) or 8‑bit with bitsandbytes.
	•	Steps: depends on dataset; for 100k pairs, aim 50–200k steps with checkpointing.

⸻

4) How to connect Q‑Former → LLM in code (practical)

Two common patterns:

A) Prefix‑embeds into LLM (causal LLMs)
	•	Compute Q‑former outputs Q = [q1..qK] (shape K×D_q)
	•	P = Linear(Q) → K×D_lm
	•	When calling causal LLM, call generate or forward with inputs_embeds = concat(P, token_embeddings_of_prompt) — note HF behaviour: some models expect positional embeddings; test carefully. See HF issue about inputs_embeds.  ￼

B) Encoder‑side injection (T5 / encoder‑decoder) — often cleaner:
	•	Treat Q‑former outputs as encoder tokens by mapping them into encoder embedding dim and concatenating with text encoder inputs (or using a small multi‑modal encoder wrapper). LAVIS BLIP‑2 uses this approach for T5 in their blip2_t5.py.  ￼

References / code to copy/adapt:
