	•	HF BLIP‑2 docs and model examples.  ￼
	•	Q‑Former standalone implementation (helpful) on GitHub.  ￼

⸻

5) Additional practicalities & improvements for UI tasks
	1.	Auxiliary detection head (recommended)
	•	Add a small detection/regression head on top of Q‑former outputs to predict precise bounding boxes (L1 loss). This helps numeric accuracy of bounds beyond tokenized coordinates. Train jointly (multi‑task loss: token CE + box L1). This combines best of detection + seq2seq.
	2.	Tokenization of coords
	•	Two options:
	•	Keep coords as strings "100,200,300,260" — simpler, but numeric precision learned tokenwise.
	•	Predict quantized coords as discrete tokens (e.g., 0–1000 mapped to tokens) — more robust but needs special tokenizer entries.
	3.	Hierarchy
	•	Train the LLM to output children arrays or parent indices to represent tree structure. Use containment heuristics as a fallback.
	4.	Data
	•	Pretrain Q‑former on image-caption and OCR tasks (BLIP‑2 style two‑stage pretrain) to bootstrap alignment. Then finetune on screenshot→JSON pairs. BLIP‑2 paper describes two stage training.  ￼
	5.	Constrained decoding
	•	Use grammar/schema checking (e.g. a JSON schema validator) to repair small tokenization mistakes.

